course: dictionary
lesson: 2220
title: Courtiers at the king's court
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A courtier is a person who spends time at the court of a king or queen. The court is the royal household, and courtiers are the advisers, friends and attendants who live and work around the monarch. Look at «ten courtiers ornamented with diamonds» in the procession: the other people with them are soldiers and royal children, so courtiers must belong to the royal court.
explain: Context clues help again. In King Canute's story, the courtiers are called «flattering courtiers», and the brackets explain that they praised the king to please him. So flatter means to praise someone, often more than they deserve, to win their favour. Swap the meaning in: "the people around him who praised him to please him" still makes sense.
explain: Courtiers are people, not things. You can have one courtier or many courtiers, so courtier is a noun and its plural is courtiers. A courtier is always a person, never a place or an object.
fact: A courtier is a person who spends time at the court of a king or queen.
fact: Courtiers can be advisers, friends or attendants.
fact: Courtiers often tried to please the monarch to win favour, as in the royal courts of the Tudors and Versailles.
fact: In «The Emperor's New Clothes», courtiers flatter the ruler.
fact: The plural of courtier is courtiers.
exercise: «Ten courtiers ornamented with diamonds» joined the procession. What is a courtier? | A child who wears a crown | A gardener who paints roses | *A person who spends time at the court of a king or queen | A soldier who carries a club
exercise: In King Canute's story, what were the flattering courtiers doing? | *Praising him to please him | Arguing with the tide | Carrying the crown on a cushion | Building a castle by the sea
exercise: Which word is the plural of courtier? | courtier's | courtiered | *courtiers | courtiery
test: In the book, the four suits have jobs. What job do diamonds have? | Gardeners | *Courtiers | Spades | Royal children
test: Why did courtiers try to please the monarch? | To make the soldiers angry | *To win favour | To sell diamonds | To become gardeners
exam: Which sentence best explains what a courtier is? | *A person who spends time at the court of a king or queen, as an adviser, friend or attendant | A soldier who carries a club | A person who gardens at the palace | A king who rules the sea
